            content: "Polonnaruwa became the island's capital in the eleventh century after Anuradhapura fell to a Chola invasion, and it lasted about two hundred years. That short window matters, because it means the city was built more or less at once, to a plan, and then abandoned — which is why it is the most coherent and best-preserved of Sri Lanka's ancient capitals.\n\nParakramabahu I, who ruled from 1153 to 1186, is the reason most of it exists. He built a seven-storey palace, a religious quarter of extraordinary density, and the Parakrama Samudra — the Sea of Parakrama — a reservoir of 2,500 hectares that still irrigates the district today. His stated principle was that no drop of rain should reach the sea without serving people first, and the hydraulic engineering here is arguably more impressive than the architecture.\n\nFor a visitor the practical difference from Anuradhapura is scale and legibility. Anuradhapura is spread over forty square kilometres and is still an active pilgrimage city. Polonnaruwa is compact, walkable in clusters, and reads as a single place. If you have one morning for an ancient capital, this is the one.",
