            icon: "landmark",
            content: "Anuradhapura was the capital of Sri Lanka from the fourth century BC until the eleventh century AD — over a thousand years, which makes it one of the longest continuously inhabited capitals in the ancient world. At its height it was a city of monasteries and reservoirs supporting tens of thousands of monks.\n\nThe crucial thing to understand before visiting is that it is not a ruin in the way Polonnaruwa is. The great dagobas are whitewashed, in use, and circled daily by pilgrims in white. The Sri Maha Bodhi has an unbroken chain of custodianship going back over two thousand years. On a poya day the site holds tens of thousands of Sri Lankans who are not there to look at anything.\n\nThat changes how you should visit. Anuradhapura rewards a slower, quieter approach than the other cultural triangle sites, and it punishes the tick-list one. Visitors who arrive with two hours and a camera generally leave saying it was less impressive than Polonnaruwa, which is true only in the sense that a cathedral during mass is less photogenic than an empty one.\n\nIt is spread over about forty square kilometres and needs a vehicle between clusters, a full day, and ideally a guide.",
